<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acemhöyük</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acemhöyük is a large settlement mound near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksaray">Aksaray</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, occupied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. Excavations since 1962 have uncovered two palaces of the 19th and 18th centuries BCE, with clay sealings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a> and goods linked to the Old Assyrian trade network. Many scholars identify it with Purushanda (Burushattum), a wealthy city named in the Assyrian merchants' letters, though the identification is debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acemhoyuk.jpg" alt="Acemhöyük" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Al-qamar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KarumKanis.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Acemhöyük is a tell near the village of Yeşilova in Merkez district, Aksaray Province, 18 km northwest of Aksaray at the southeastern end of the Tuz Gölü, in a fertile plain on the Uluirmak or Melendiz. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kültepe</a>, it has two parts: a settlement on a hill about 700 m by 600 m, whose citadel rises 20 metres and is called Sarikaya, the yellow cliff, after its gleaming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbricks</a>, and a lower city partly covered by the modern village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mound has several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> levels, from Early Bronze II (2700–2450 BCE) to the end of Early Bronze III (2450–2000 BCE). At that time Acemhöyük was a central hub of the Anatolian Trade Network, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to the east. Imported Syrian bottles, small flasks probably used for medicinal oils or perfumes, show its links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> the town belonged to the Assyrian trade network, with a trading post, or karum. Its Level III marks the height of its prosperity; it was destroyed by a fire of unknown cause, and settlement on the hill ceased until the Hellenistic or Roman period. On the citadel stood the Sarıkaya Palace, of about 50 rooms, with walls 1.5 to 2 metres thick and a portico of marble bases and wooden pillars. Radiocarbon and tree-ring study of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> in 2016 left only the middle or low-middle chronologies of the period possible.</p><p>Clay bullae from the palace carry seal impressions of Dugedu, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim">Yahdun-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>, and of King Aplahanda of Carchemish, 16 of them from his two seals. A label and a small tablet in Old Assyrian, the first tablet found at the site, came to light in 2012/2013 and date from around 1700 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Excavation began in 1962 under the General Directorate of Antiquities and Museums with the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, led by Nimet Özgüç until 1988 and by Aliye Öztan from 1989. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory</a> furniture fittings given to the Metropolitan Museum of Art between 1932 and 1937 by George D. Pratt, parts of a gold and ivory throne reconstructed by Elizabeth Simpson, match ivories excavated in the palace and were looted from the site. Other finds are in the Aksaray Museum and the Niğde Archaeological Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acemhöyük” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burushattum appears in Old Assyrian trade letters of the 20th and 19th centuries BCE as a wealthy city in south-central Anatolia, probably at <b>Acemhöyük</b> near Aksaray, though the identification is debated.</blockquote><a href="/state/purushanda">Purushanda</a></li></ul></section>
